<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banffshire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as County of Banff</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banffshire is a historic county of north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, stretching from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a> coast at Banff and Macduff south into the Cairngorm mountains, including much of the Spey valley. It was used for local government until 1975 and is now divided between the Moray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a> council areas. Its lowlands were farming and fishing country, and its Speyside glens are known for whisky distilling.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banffshire.jpg" alt="Banffshire" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brythones; derivative work by of Visitor from Wikishire · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banffshire_County.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banffshire had a 30-mile stretch of coast on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a> from Spey Bay to Cullykhan Bay, with its hinterland and a long, tapering tail running some 55 miles inland along the Spey into the Grampian Mountains. It bordered Moray and Inverness-shire to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a> to the east and south. The county town was Banff, while the largest settlement was Buckie. The area holds evidence of prehistoric settlement near the coast, such as the cairns at Longman Hill and Cairn Lee, as well as ruined medieval castles and the 12th-century kirk of Gamrie.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The origins of the shire, the area run by a sheriff, are obscure: there is some evidence that it existed under David I, who reigned from 1124 to 1153, but the first documented Sheriff of Banff dates from the 13th century. The sheriff's jurisdiction covered small provincial lordships lying between the larger provinces of Moray and Buchan, among them Boyne and Enzie on the coast and Strathisla, Glenlivet, Strathavon and Glenfiddich inland. In 1242 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a> granted five properties of the king's forest of Banff to Waleran de Normanville.</p><p>Commissioners of Supply governed the shire from 1667, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union</a> of 1707 the English word county came to be used beside shire. An elected county council first met on 22 May 1890 at Banff Sheriff Court. Boundary changes that took effect on 15 May 1891 removed four detached parts surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a>, the largest being the parish of St Fergus. In 1975 the county was placed in the Grampian region and divided between the districts of Banff and Buchan and Moray.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Since 1996 the area has been shared between the Moray and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeenshire">Aberdeenshire</a> council areas. The pre-1975 boundaries are still used as a registration county for land registration and as a lieutenancy area. Its towns include Banff, Buckie, Keith, Cullen, Dufftown, Macduff, Portsoy and Tomintoul, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inverness">Inverness</a> railway runs through Keith.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banffshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
